"
\h0#preface|Preface|

This document describes \c{libgzip}, a C++ library for compressing and
decompressing data in the gzip file format. For the details of the library
interface refer to the comments in its public headers. For the format itself
see \l{https://www.rfc-editor.org/info/rfc1952 RFC 1952}.


\h1#intro|Introduction|

The \c{libgzip} library is a C++ implementation of the gzip file format as
specified by RFC 1952. A gzip file is a series of members, each of which holds
deflate data (RFC 1951) between a header and a trailer. The header describes
the original data, for example, with its file name and modification time, and
the trailer holds the CRC-32 and the size of the original data, which the
decompressor verifies. The deflate data itself is compressed and decompressed
by the \l{https://iw4x.io/projects/libdeflate/doc/ libdeflate} library, and
\c{libgzip} provides the same interfaces on top of it: an incremental
compressor and decompressor, functions that work on the whole data at once,
and \c{iostream} adapters.

The decompressor accepts every file that the format defines and rejects
everything else, including a few files that other decompressors let through
(see \l{#conformance Conformance} for details).

We begin with a tutorial introduction that builds a small compression utility,
once with each of the library's interfaces, and along the way gives the
compressed data a header that names the original file. The remaining chapters
then cover the details that the tutorial passes over.


\h#intro-setup|Project Setup|

Our utility is called \c{gz}. It compresses its standard input to its
standard output and, with the \c{-d} option, decompresses instead. Let's start
by creating a new executable project for it (here we assume our current
working directory is \c{/tmp}):

\
$ bdep new -l c++ -t exe gz
created new executable project gz in /tmp/gz/

$ cd gz
\

To use \c{libgzip} in our project, we first edit the
\c{repositories.manifest} file found in the root directory of the project and
add the \c{libgzip} repository as a prerequisite:

\
role: prerequisite
location: https://github.com/iw4x-x64-stage/libgzip.git#main
\

Next, we edit the \c{manifest} file (again, found in the root of our project)
and specify the dependency on \c{libgzip}:

\
depends: libgzip ^0.1.0-a.0.1
\

\N|The \c{-a.0.1} part of the constraint lets it match the snapshots of
\c{libgzip} that precede its \c{0.1.0} release, which are the only versions
available until then. Once \c{0.1.0} is released, the constraint becomes
\c{^0.1.0}.|

Finally, we import the library in \c{gz/buildfile}. We won't be writing
tests in this tutorial, so we also remove \c{testscript} that came with the
project, here and in the \c{gz/} directory:

\
$ cat gz/buildfile

libs =
import libs += libgzip%lib{gzip}

exe{gz}: {hxx ixx txx cxx}{**} $libs

cxx.poptions =+ \"-I$out_root\" \"-I$src_root\"
\

\N|If these files are new to you, see
\l{https://build2.org/build2-toolchain/doc/build2-toolchain-intro.xhtml#guide-add-remove-deps
Adding and Removing Dependencies} in the \c{build2} toolchain introduction for
the details on each of them.|

With the dependency in place, let's initialize our project in a build
configuration:

\
$ bdep init -C @gcc cc config.cxx=g++
initializing in project /tmp/gz/
created configuration @gcc /tmp/gz-gcc/ 1 target
    default,forwarded,auto-synchronized
fetching git:github.com/iw4x-x64-stage/libgzip#main (prerequisite
    of dir:/tmp/gz)
fetching git:github.com/iw4x-x64-stage/libdeflate#main (prerequisite
    of git:github.com/iw4x-x64-stage/libgzip#main)
...
synchronizing:
  new libdeflate/0.1.0-a.0.20261029235224.fab77466e02b (required
    by libgzip)
  new libgzip/0.1.0-a.0... (required by gz)
  new gz/0.1.0-a.0.19700101000000
\

As you can see, \c{bdep} fetched the \c{libgzip} repository together with the
\c{libdeflate} repository, which \c{libgzip}'s repository lists as its own
prerequisite, and configured both libraries in our build configuration along
with \c{gz}. We are now ready to write some code.


\h#intro-whole|Whole Data|

The simplest way to use \c{libgzip} is to give it the whole data at once.
Here is the first version of \c{gz}, which works this way:

\
$ cat gz/gz.cxx

#include <print>
#include <string>
#include <iostream>
#include <iterator>
#include <string_view>

#include <libgzip/compressor.hxx>
#include <libgzip/decompressor.hxx>

using namespace std;

int
main (int argc, char* argv[])
{
  const bool d (argc > 1 && argv[1] == string_view (\"-d\"));

  const string in (istreambuf_iterator<char> (cin), {});

  try
  {
    print (\"{}\", d ? gzip::decompress (in) : gzip::compress (in));
  }
  catch (const gzip::invalid_data& e)
  {
    println (cerr, \"error: {}\", e.what ());
    return 1;
  }
}
\

The \c{gzip::compress()} function is declared in
\c{<libgzip/compressor.hxx>} and \c{gzip::decompress()} in
\c{<libgzip/decompressor.hxx>}. Both take the data as \c{std::string_view}
and return the result as \c{std::string}. Everything in the library is in
the \c{gzip} namespace.

Let's build our program and try it on a list of numbers:

\
$ b
c++ gz/cxx{gz} -> ../gz-gcc/gz/gz/obje{gz}
c++ ../gz-gcc/libgzip-.../libgzip/cxx{decompressor} -> ...
...
ld ../gz-gcc/libdeflate-.../libdeflate/libs{deflate}
ld ../gz-gcc/libgzip-.../libgzip/libs{gzip}
ld ../gz-gcc/gz/gz/exe{gz}
ln ../gz-gcc/gz/gz/exe{gz} -> gz/

$ seq 1 100000 >numbers.txt

$ gz/gz <numbers.txt >numbers.txt.gz
$ gz/gz -d <numbers.txt.gz | cmp - numbers.txt

$ wc -c numbers.txt numbers.txt.gz
588895 numbers.txt
212863 numbers.txt.gz
801758 total
\

The decompressed data is the same as the original (\c{cmp} prints nothing)
and the compressed data takes a little over a third of its size. Of its
212863 bytes, 18 are the header and the trailer of the one member that
\c{compress()} writes and the rest is the deflate data. Since our data is in
the gzip format, the \c{gzip} utility can decompress it as well, and our
program can decompress what \c{gzip} compresses:

\
$ gzip -dc numbers.txt.gz | cmp - numbers.txt
$ gzip -c numbers.txt | gz/gz -d | cmp - numbers.txt
\

\N|Note that we gave our data file the \c{.txt} extension. The root
directory of our project is in the header search paths (that
\c{cxx.poptions} line in \c{gz/buildfile}), and a file called \c{numbers}
there would take the place of the standard \c{<numbers>} header that other
standard headers include.|

Let's now see what happens if we try to decompress something that is not
gzip data, such as our list of numbers:

\
$ gz/gz -d <numbers.txt
error: expected identification byte 0x1f instead of 0x31
\

When \c{decompress()} encounters a fault in the data, it throws
\c{gzip::invalid_data} with the description of the fault as the message.
Every member starts with the two identification bytes \c{0x1f} and
\c{0x8b}, and our file starts with the digit \c{1}, which is \c{0x31}. The
decompressor checks each byte of the header as soon as it gets to it, so it
reports this fault at the first byte.

The \c{invalid_data} exception is derived from \c{std::invalid_argument}.
The faults that \c{decompress()} reports with it include those that
\c{libdeflate} finds in the deflate data of a member, and it also throws it
if the data ends before its last member does. For example:

\
$ head -c 1000 numbers.txt.gz | gz/gz -d
error: incomplete deflate data
\

The data of a gzip file is the data of all of its members, one after another.
So if we concatenate two files, we get a file with two members, which
decompresses into the data of both:

\
$ cat numbers.txt.gz numbers.txt.gz | gz/gz -d | wc -c
1177790
\

\N|Compression accepts any data and therefore never throws
\c{invalid_data}. It may throw \c{std::invalid_argument} for an invalid
header, which we will get to in \l{#intro-names File Names}.|

This version of \c{gz} works but it keeps both the input and the output in
memory, which limits the size of the data it can handle. Let's fix that with
streams.


\h#intro-streams|Streams|

The \c{<libgzip/stream.hxx>} header provides two stream classes:
\c{gzip::ostream} compresses what we write to it into another output stream
and \c{gzip::istream} decompresses what it reads from another input stream.
Here is the second version of our program:

\
$ cat gz/gz.cxx

#include <print>
#include <iostream>
#include <string_view>

#include <libgzip/stream.hxx>

using namespace std;

int
main (int argc, char* argv[])
{
  const bool d (argc > 1 && argv[1] == string_view (\"-d\"));

  cin.exceptions (ios::badbit);
  cout.exceptions (ios::badbit | ios::failbit);

  try
  {
    if (d)
    {
      gzip::istream is (cin);

      if (is.peek () != istream::traits_type::eof ())
        cout << is.rdbuf ();
    }
    else
    {
      gzip::ostream os (cout);

      if (cin.peek () != istream::traits_type::eof ())
        os << cin.rdbuf ();

      os.close ();
    }
  }
  catch (const gzip::invalid_data& e)
  {
    println (cerr, \"error: {}\", e.what ());
    return 1;
  }
}
\

Let's start with the compressing side. The \c{gzip::ostream} constructor
takes the stream to write the compressed data to and, optionally, the
compression level and the header. The compressed data is complete only after
\c{close()} writes the rest of the deflate data and the trailer, so we call
it explicitly (see \l{#contracts Programming Errors} for what happens if we
forget). The errors of writing to \c{cout} come through \c{gzip::ostream} as
exceptions, which is why it requires the other stream to have the
\c{badbit} and \c{failbit} exceptions enabled.

On the decompressing side, the gzip data must end the other stream, so
anything after the last member is an error. The \c{invalid_data} exception
comes through \c{gzip::istream} the same as from \c{decompress()}, which
is why \c{gzip::istream} requires the \c{badbit} exception, which it
enables by default.

We only insert the stream buffers if they have something to insert. If the
stream buffer that we insert with \c{operator<<} has no characters, the
insertion sets \c{failbit} on the stream that we insert into, and both
\c{cout} and \c{gzip::ostream} have the \c{failbit} exception enabled.

Let's build and try it:

\
$ b
c++ gz/cxx{gz} -> ../gz-gcc/gz/gz/obje{gz}
ld ../gz-gcc/gz/gz/exe{gz}
ln ../gz-gcc/gz/gz/exe{gz} -> gz/

$ gz/gz <numbers.txt >numbers.txt.gz
$ gz/gz -d <numbers.txt.gz | cmp - numbers.txt

$ wc -c numbers.txt.gz
212863 numbers.txt.gz

$ (cat numbers.txt.gz; echo junk) | gz/gz -d >/dev/null
error: expected identification byte 0x1f instead of 0x6a
\

As you can see, after the end of the first member the decompressor expects
another member and finds the letter \c{j} instead. Empty data compresses into
a member as well:

\
$ gz/gz </dev/null | od -An -tx1
 1f 8b 08 00 00 00 00 00 00 ff 03 00 00 00 00 00
 00 00 00 00

$ gz/gz </dev/null | gz/gz -d | wc -c
0
\

The first ten bytes are the header: the identification bytes, the
compression method (8, deflate), the flags, the modification time, the extra
flags, and the operating system (255, unknown). Then come the two bytes of
the deflate data, which is a single block that contains nothing but its end,
and the trailer with the CRC-32 and the size of the empty data, both zero.

Now our \c{gz} can handle data of any size. Both streams keep a buffer on
each side and the compressor keeps a window of the data it searches for
matches, and that is all the memory they need. Let's compress 100MB of
zeros and see how much memory it takes:

\
$ head -c 100000000 /dev/zero | \\
  /usr/bin/time -f '%M KB' gz/gz | wc -c
5392 KB
97217
\

Notice that the 5MB that our program takes includes the C++ runtime and the
two libraries themselves. With ten times as much data we see about the same
figure.

\N|On Windows the standard streams are opened in the text mode, which
translates line endings and so corrupts binary data. A real \c{gz} would
switch \c{stdin} and \c{stdout} to the binary mode with \c{_setmode()}
first.|


\h#intro-names|File Names|

So far our members have the default header, which says nothing about the
original data. The \c{gzip} utility records the name of the original file
and its modification time, and we can do the same with \c{gzip::header},
declared in \c{<libgzip/header.hxx>}. Let's extend the streams version of
\c{gz} with the \c{-n} option, which records the file name that follows it
and the current time, and with the \c{-l} option, which prints the file name
and the modification time of the first member instead of the data:

\
$ cat gz/gz.cxx

#include <ctime>
#include <print>
#include <chrono>
#include <cstdint>
#include <iostream>
#include <string_view>

#include <libgzip/stream.hxx>

using namespace std;

int
main (int argc, char* argv[])
{
  const string_view m (argc > 1 ? argv[1] : \"\");

  cin.exceptions (ios::badbit);
  cout.exceptions (ios::badbit | ios::failbit);

  try
  {
    if (m == \"-d\" || m == \"-l\")
    {
      gzip::istream is (cin);

      const bool empty (is.peek () == istream::traits_type::eof ());

      if (m == \"-l\")
      {
        const gzip::header& h (*is.header ());
        const chrono::sys_seconds t (chrono::seconds (h.mtime));

        println (\"{} {:%F %T}\", h.name.value_or (\"-\"), t);
      }
      else if (!empty)
        cout << is.rdbuf ();
    }
    else
    {
      gzip::header h;
      h.mtime = static_cast<uint32_t> (time (nullptr));

      if (m == \"-n\" && argc > 2)
        h.name = argv[2];

      gzip::ostream os (cout, gzip::compression_level::balanced, h);

      if (cin.peek () != istream::traits_type::eof ())
        os << cin.rdbuf ();

      os.close ();
    }
  }
  catch (const gzip::invalid_data& e)
  {
    println (cerr, \"error: {}\", e.what ());
    return 1;
  }
}
\

The header is a plain struct whose members are the header values, each with
a default that makes the default header. The \c{mtime} member is the
modification time in seconds since the epoch, which the format limits to 32
bits, and the \c{name} member is a \c{std::optional<std::string>}, since the
format distinguishes a member without a file name from one with an empty
name. We pass the header to the \c{gzip::ostream} constructor after the
level, which we spell out here with its default value.

On the decompressing side, \c{header()} returns the header of the member
that the characters being read come from. It has a value once the stream has
read something, which \c{peek()} makes it do. Since the data has at least one
member, there is a header even if the data is empty.

Let's build our program and record the name of our list of numbers:

\
$ b
c++ gz/cxx{gz} -> ../gz-gcc/gz/gz/obje{gz}
ld ../gz-gcc/gz/gz/exe{gz}
ln ../gz-gcc/gz/gz/exe{gz} -> gz/

$ gz/gz -n numbers.txt <numbers.txt >named.gz
$ gz/gz -l <named.gz
numbers.txt 2026-10-30 02:31:41

$ gzip -lN named.gz
         compressed        uncompressed  ratio uncompressed_name
             212875              588895  63.9% numbers.txt
\

As you can see, \c{gzip} finds the name that we recorded. The member is 12
bytes larger than before: the file name with its terminating zero byte. Our
program can also see what \c{gzip} records:

\
$ touch -d '2026-10-29 12:00:00 UTC' numbers.txt

$ gzip -c numbers.txt | gz/gz -l
numbers.txt 2026-10-29 12:00:00

$ gzip -nc numbers.txt | gz/gz -l
- 1970-01-01 00:00:00
\

With the \c{-n} option \c{gzip} leaves the name and the time out, and the
header says so with the time stamp 0 and no file name.

The header has more to it than the file name and the time, and the format
puts a few requirements on its values (see \l{#headers Headers} for details).
For example, the file name may not contain a zero byte, since a zero byte
terminates it in the header. A header that doesn't meet them makes the
compressor throw \c{std::invalid_argument}. Our program has no handler for
it, since a command line argument cannot contain a zero byte.


\h#intro-incremental|Incremental Interface|

Streams are convenient when the data comes from a stream and goes to one.
When it comes from somewhere else, for example, a network connection or a
memory-mapped file, we can use the compressor and decompressor directly. Here
is the last version of our program, which does that:

\
$ cat gz/gz.cxx

#include <print>
#include <iostream>
#include <string_view>

#include <libgzip/compressor.hxx>
#include <libgzip/decompressor.hxx>

using namespace std;

int
main (int argc, char* argv[])
{
  const bool d (argc > 1 && argv[1] == string_view (\"-d\"));

  cin.exceptions (ios::badbit);
  cout.exceptions (ios::badbit | ios::failbit);

  auto run = [] (auto& c)
  {
    char in[4096];
    char out[4096];

    while (!c.finished ())
    {
      cin.read (in, sizeof (in));

      string_view i (in, static_cast<size_t> (cin.gcount ()));
      const bool end (cin.eof ());

      for (gzip::progress p {0, sizeof (out)};
           !c.finished () &&
           (!i.empty () || p.produced == sizeof (out)); )
      {
        p = c.next (i, out, end);
        i.remove_prefix (p.consumed);

        cout.write (out, static_cast<streamsize> (p.produced));
      }
    }
  };

  try
  {
    if (d)
    {
      gzip::decompressor x;
      run (x);
    }
    else
    {
      gzip::compressor c;
      run (c);
    }
  }
  catch (const gzip::invalid_data& e)
  {
    println (cerr, \"error: {}\", e.what ());
    return 1;
  }
}
\

Let's unpack the \c{run} lambda. The \c{gzip::compressor} and
\c{gzip::decompressor} classes have the same interface as their
\c{libdeflate} counterparts, so one lambda handles both. The work is done by
the \c{next()} member function, which takes the next part of the input, the
output buffer, and whether this part is the last one. It returns a
\c{gzip::progress} value with the number of input bytes it \c{consumed} and
the number of output bytes it \c{produced}, which we use to advance the input
and to write the output.

The outer loop reads the input in parts and the inner loop hands each part to
\c{next()}. Notice that the inner loop keeps calling \c{next()} after the
input is used up if the previous call filled the output buffer. A few bits of
deflate data can stand for hundreds of bytes of output, so a full output
buffer means there may be more output to write for the input that was already
consumed. The inner loop also keeps calling \c{next()} while there is input
left, since the decompressor stops at the end of each member.

The \c{end} argument says that the data ends within this part. Our program
learns that when \c{cin} reaches the end of its input. For the decompressor,
\c{end} means that the data must end within this part, and if the input runs
out before its last member does, \c{next()} throws \c{invalid_data}. Until
then, the end of a member may be followed by another one, so the
decompressor only finishes once it knows there is no more input. For the
compressor, \c{end} means that there is no more data to compress, so it can
finish the deflate data and write the trailer once it has consumed this
part. Until then the compressor holds on to the data that later input may
still find matches in, and a call to \c{next()} may consume all of its input
and produce nothing. Once we pass \c{end} as \c{true}, we must keep passing
\c{true} in the calls that follow.

Both classes report the end with \c{finished()}, which for the decompressor
means that it has reached the end of the data and for the compressor that it
has written all of it.

Let's build our last version and compare its result with the one of the
first two versions:

\
$ b
c++ gz/cxx{gz} -> ../gz-gcc/gz/gz/obje{gz}
ld ../gz-gcc/gz/gz/exe{gz}
ln ../gz-gcc/gz/gz/exe{gz} -> gz/

$ gz/gz <numbers.txt | cmp - numbers.txt.gz
$ gz/gz -d <numbers.txt.gz | cmp - numbers.txt
\

The compressed data is the same as what the first two versions produced. The
compressor output depends only on the input, the compression level, and the
header, so it doesn't matter how we split the input into parts.

Both classes also provide \c{reset()}, which brings an object back to its
initial state so that we can reuse it for other data. Note also that after
\c{next()} throws, the decompressor can only be reset, and that the output
written by the call that throws is not reported.

Since a call to \c{next()} stops at the end of each member, the output of a
call always comes from a single member. After the call, \c{header()} returns
the header of that member and \c{members()} the number of members whose
header has been read so far. A program that needs the header of each member,
for example, to list them, can watch \c{members()} change from one call to
the next.



\h1#headers|Headers|

The header of a member is represented by \c{gzip::header}, a struct with the
following members:

\
bool             text  = false;
uint32_t         mtime = 0;
extra_flags      flags = extra_flags::none;
operating_system os    = operating_system::unknown;

optional<vector<subfield>> extra;
optional<string>           name;
optional<string>           comment;

bool crc16 = false;
\

The \c{text} member says that the data is probably ASCII text, which is the
\c{FTEXT} flag of the format. It is only a hint, which the compressor leaves
to its caller and the decompressor passes on as it is. The \c{mtime} member
is the modification time of the original data in seconds since the epoch, or
0 if the time is unknown.

The extra flags say how the deflate data was compressed. The deflate method
defines two of them, \c{maximum} for the maximum compression with the
slowest algorithm and \c{fastest} for the fastest algorithm. The compressor
sets them from the compression level: \c{maximum} for level 9, \c{fastest}
for levels 0 and 1, and \c{none} otherwise. It ignores the extra flags of the
header that it is given, which therefore only matter in the headers that the
decompressor reads.

The operating system is the type of file system on which the compression
took place, with the enumerators of \c{operating_system} naming the values
that the format defines, from \c{fat} (0) to \c{acorn_riscos} (13), and
\c{unknown} (255). The format calls these values the currently defined ones,
which leaves the list open, and the decompressor accepts any value. Note that
the value 3 is called \c{unix_system}, since GCC predefines \c{unix} as a
macro in its GNU modes.

The \c{extra}, \c{name}, and \c{comment} members are the optional fields of
the header, each present in the header if it has a value. The extra field is
a series of subfields, each a two-byte ID and up to 65531 bytes of data. The
whole field, with four bytes of ID and length for each subfield, may take up
to 65535 bytes. For example, to give a member the \c{AP} subfield with two
bytes of data:

\
gzip::header h;
h.extra = {gzip::subfield {{'A', 'P'}, \"\\x01\\x02\"}};
\

The file name and the file comment are in ISO 8859-1 and can contain any
character but the zero byte, which terminates them in the header. The file
name is the name of the original file with any directory components removed
(and, for a file system with case-insensitive names, in lower case), and the
comment separates lines with a single line feed character. The library
leaves the choice of the name and the encoding to its caller, and the
compressor writes the name and comment that it is given.

Finally, the \c{crc16} member says whether the header ends with a CRC16 of
the bytes that precede it, which is the lower half of their CRC-32. The
compressor calculates it and the decompressor verifies it.

The compressor verifies the header on construction and throws
\c{std::invalid_argument} if the file name or file comment contains a zero
byte, if the extra field is longer than 65535 bytes, or if one of its
subfields has a reserved ID, which is an ID whose second byte is zero.

The format puts no limit on the size of the file name and the file comment,
which the decompressor keeps in memory. So that the memory it takes stays
bounded, it rejects a file name or a file comment longer than its limit,
which by default is 65535 bytes, the limit of the extra field. A different
limit can be passed to the \c{decompressor} constructor and to the
\c{gzip::istream} constructor. For example:

\
gzip::decompressor d (255);

gzip::istream is (cin, 255);
\

The CRC-32 of the trailer, from which the header CRC16 is also derived, is
the CRC of ISO 3309 and ITU-T V.42. The library calculates it with
\c{gzip::crc32}, declared in \c{<libgzip/crc32.hxx>}, which is also available
to its users:

\
gzip::crc32 c;
c.append (\"1234\");
c.append (\"56789\");

assert (c.value () == 0xcbf43926);
\


\h1#levels|Compression Levels|

The compression level controls how much time the deflate compressor spends
looking for matches. It is a value of the \c{gzip::compression_level}
enumeration in the range from 0 to 9, which is \c{libdeflate}'s
\c{deflate::compression_level} under the \c{gzip} name, with the following
enumerators naming the usual levels:

\
store     0  Stored blocks only, no search for matches.
fastest   1  Shortest search.
balanced  6  The default.
smallest  9  Longest search.
\

Any other value in the range is a level as well. To compress at a level other
than the default, we pass it to \c{compress()}, to the \c{compressor}
constructor, or to the \c{gzip::ostream} constructor, each time before the
header. For example:

\
string c (gzip::compress (in, gzip::compression_level::smallest));

gzip::compressor x (static_cast<gzip::compression_level> (4), h);

gzip::ostream os (cout, gzip::compression_level::fastest);
\

See the \c{libdeflate} manual for the details on the levels and on the
deflate data that the compressor writes at each of them.


\h1#conformance|Conformance|

The decompressor accepts every file that RFC 1952 defines and rejects
everything else. Specifically, it throws \c{invalid_data} on the first of the
following faults:

\ul|

\li|Data without members.|

\li|A member that doesn't start with the identification bytes \c{0x1f} and
\c{0x8b}, which includes anything after the last member.|

\li|A compression method other than deflate (8). The format reserves the
methods from 0 to 7 and leaves the rest undefined.|

\li|A reserved flag bit set. The format requires the decompressor to reject
these, since they could indicate a field that it doesn't know how to skip
over.|

\li|Extra flags other than 0 and the two that the deflate method defines, 2
and 4.|

\li|An extra field that isn't a series of whole subfields.|

\li|A subfield with a reserved ID, which is one whose second byte is zero.|

\li|A header CRC16 that doesn't match the header.|

\li|A fault in the deflate data of a member, which \c{libdeflate} reports
(see the \c{libdeflate} manual for the faults it finds).|

\li|A CRC-32 or a size in the trailer that doesn't match the decompressed
data. The size is that of the data of the member modulo 2^32.|

\li|Data that ends before its last member does.|

|

It also rejects a member whose file name or file comment is longer than its
limit (see \l{#headers Headers} for details). This is the only fault that it
finds in data that the format defines.

The decompressor accepts any operating system value, since the format leaves
the list of values open, and it accepts an empty extra field, an empty file
name, and a member with empty data, which the format defines. It passes
the text flag, the file name, and the file comment on as they are.

The compressor only writes data that the decompressor accepts. It writes a
single member, with the extra flags that its level calls for and the reserved
flag bits zero, and it rejects the headers that the format doesn't allow
(see \l{#headers Headers} for details).


\h1#contracts|Programming Errors|

Using the library in a way that its documentation rules out is a programming
error, which the library reports as a contract violation. Recall that in the
streams version of \c{gz} we call \c{close()} on \c{gzip::ostream}
explicitly. Let's see what happens if we forget:

\
$ gz/gz <numbers.txt >/dev/null
stream.cxx:236: error: precondition violated: !is_open () ||
    !good () || uncaught_exceptions () != 0
stream.cxx:236: note: in '~ostream'
\

A \c{gzip::ostream} that is destroyed while still open would leave the
compressed data incomplete, so it treats this as a programming error unless
the stream is no longer good or an exception is being thrown.

By default, a contract violation prints a report like this to \c{stderr} and
aborts the process. To handle it differently, for example, to throw an
exception in a test, we can install our own handler with
\c{gzip::set_contract_handler()}, declared in \c{<libgzip/contract.hxx>}.
The handler receives a \c{gzip::contract_violation} that describes the failed
check. If the handler returns, the default report is printed and the process
is aborted all the same.

Note that \c{libdeflate} checks its own contracts in the same way, with its
own handler, which \c{deflate::set_contract_handler()} installs.

The amount of checking is selected when building the library with the
\c{LIBGZIP_CONTRACT} macro: \c{0} omits the checks, \c{1} (the default)
checks preconditions and assertions, and \c{2} adds postconditions and
invariants. For example, to run the library's tests at the highest level:

\
$ b test config.cxx.poptions=-DLIBGZIP_CONTRACT=2
\
"
